### Why is blocking IO on commonPool dangerous?

ForkJoinPool.commonPool() has only cores - 1 threads. These threads are shared across the entire JVM. When blocking IO consumes all threads, nothing else can run.

```java
// Component A: 3 slow JDBC queries on commonPool
for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
    CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> {
        return jdbcQuery("SELECT ..."); // blocks 2 seconds!
    });
}
// All 3 commonPool threads blocked for 2 seconds!

// Component B: parallel stream (UNRELATED, also uses commonPool)
accounts.parallelStream()         // STALLED!
    .map(this::generateStatement)  // No threads available!
    .collect(Collectors.toList());

// Component C: CF notification (UNRELATED)
CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> sendNotification()); // QUEUED!
```

### Who shares commonPool?

- `CompletableFuture.supplyAsync()` (no executor parameter)
- `parallelStream()`
- `Arrays.parallelSort()`
- Many frameworks (Spring internals, etc.)

All compete for the same few threads.

### How do you fix it?

```java
// Dedicated pools per concern
ExecutorService jdbcPool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(20);
ExecutorService notifyPool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(10);

CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> jdbcQuery(), jdbcPool);     // on jdbcPool
CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> sendNotif(), notifyPool);   // on notifyPool
accounts.parallelStream().map(...);  // commonPool free for this!
```

